# Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs)-Derived Nitrogen-Doped Porous Carbon Anchored on Graphene with Multifunctional Effects for Lithium-Sulfur Batteries

Lithium–sulfur (Li–S) batteries are highly appealing for next-generation electrochemical energy storage owing to their high theoretical energy density, environmental friendliness, and low cost. However, the insulating nature of sulfur and migration of dissolved polysulfide intermediates lead to low active material utilization and fast capacity decay, which pose a significant challenge to their practical applications. Here, this paper reports a multifunctional carbon hybrid with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived nitrogen-doped porous carbon anchored on graphene sheets (NPC/G) serving as a sulfur host. On the one hand, the high surface area and nitrogen-doping of the carbon nanoparticles enable effective polysulfide immobilization through both physical confinement and chemical adsorption; on the other hand, the highly conductive graphene provides an interconnected conductive framework to facilitate fast electron transport, improving the sulfur utilization. As a result, the NPC/G-based sulfur cathode exhibits a high specific capacity of $\mathsf{1}372\mathsf{m A h}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ with good cycling stability over 300 cycles. This approach provides a promising approach for the design of MOFs-derived carbon materials for high performance Li–S batteries.
## 1. Introduction
Lithium–sulfur (Li–S) battery has become a promising candidate for next generation energy storage systems due to the advantages of high theoretical capacity $(1675\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ , nearly five times that of the state-of-the-art lithium-ion batteries), natural abundance and environmental friendliness.[1,2] The applications of Li–S batteries bid fair to overcome the over-reliance of fossil energy, reduce exhaust emission, alleviate air pollution, and break through the bottleneck of the next generation energy storage devices using in electric vehicles and hybrid electric vehicles, for instance. However, the commercial application of Li–S batteries is hindered by several challenges: (1) the poor electrical conductivity of elemental sulfur and its final discharge product of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ lead to sluggish electrochemical reaction kinetics and result in low active material utilization; (2) the large volume expansion $(\approx80\%)$ of sulfur during the lithiation process destroys the integrity of cathode structure and leads to electrical isolation of active materials with the conductive matrix, resulting in fast capacity decay; (3) diffusion of soluble polysulfide intermediates causes the known “shuttle phenomenon,” leading to irreversible loss of active materials and corrosion of the lithium anode; (4) the use of metallic lithium anode causes safety concerns due to the formation of uncontrolled lithium dendrites.[1–4]
To solve the problems mentioned above, much effort has been made in the design of cathode materials, modification of separators, development of new electrolytes, and protection of the lithium anode.[5–8] For the sulfur cathode, the most common strategy is to encapsulate sulfur into an electrically conductive matrix to improve the conductivity of the cathode, alleviate the migration of polysulfides, and accommodate the volume changes.[9] In this regard, carbon materials have been extensively investigated due to the merits of high conductivity, abundant pore structure, tunable surface properties, and lightweight. Various sulfur/carbon composite materials have been explored with the use of porous carbon,[10,11] graphene,[12,13] carbon nanotubes,[14–16] carbon fibers,[17,18] and their hybrid materials,[19–23] resulting in significantly improved electrochemical performances. More recently, considering the different chemical bonding nature of carbon (nonpolar) and polysulfides (polar), the relatively weak interactions between polysulfides and the $\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ -structured carbon plane are considered to be insufficient to immobilize the migrating polysulfides. Therefore, increasing attentions have been paid to the modification of the surface properties of carbon materials by functionalization, e.g., nitrogen doping, which has proved to be an effective way in enhancing the interaction of polysulfides with carbon matrixes.[24]
Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived carbon materials, with abundant and controllable pore structures and intrinsic heteroatom doping, have gained much attention when using as sulfur host materials, as they show dual confinement effect of polysulfides by both physical encapsulation and chemical adsorption.[25–29] Two typical MOFs, zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) and zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67), which contain zinc and cobalt, respectively, have been widely investigated in recent years. Lou and co-workers[26] reported the use of ZIF-8-derived microporous carbon polyhedrons as a sulfur matrix, which exhibited a high initial capacity of $1500\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ . Dong and co-workers[30] synthesized a ZIF-67-derived sulfur host containing cobalt and nitrogendoped graphitic carbon, which served as an electronic matrix to entrap polysulfides, resulting in good cycling stabilities over 500 cycles at $1\mathrm{C}$ . Despite the advantages, challenges remain in two aspects: first, the electrical conductivities of MOFs-derived carbon materials are not high enough due to the relatively low graphitic crystallinity; second, MOFs-derived carbon materials tend to aggregate together due to the high surface energy of nanosized particles, which leads to not only sluggish lithium ion transport but also insufficient contact with the dissolved polysulfides. In this regard, constructing composite structures with MOFs-derived carbon uniformly dispersed in a conductive matrix is expected to be an effective way to improve both the electron and lithium ion transport capabilities.
Graphene, a unique atom-thick layer consisting of $\displaystyle\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ - bonded carbon atoms, is well known to have high surface area, superior conductivity, excellent mechanical properties and good flexibility. Moreover, the nanosheet morphology of graphene offers a highly accessible surface with abundant exposed active sites, which provides opportunities for the construction of various graphene-based hybrid structures. Tour and co-workers[31] incorporated sulfur into a polyaniline (PANI) matrix with graphene as the conductive skeleton. It was suggested that PANI and graphene can form synergistic effects to confine the migrating polysulfides, resulting in enhanced cycling stabilities. Al Salem et  al.[32] fabricated graphene-based nanocomposites with Pt and Ni nanoparticles uniformly anchored on graphene, which shows electrocatalytic effect toward the electrochemical transformation of long-chain polyusulfides during cycling, resulting in reduced overpotential and excellent specific capacity. Herein, we report the in situ growth of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 on the graphene oxide, resulting in a nitrogen-doped porous carbon/graphene (NPC/G) hybrid after thermal treatment. The highly conductive graphene not only provides an interconnected conductive framework to facilitate fast electron transport, but also serves as a building unit to support the MOFs-derived carbon. The well-dispersed MOFs-derived nitrogen-doped porous carbon gives rise to both physical confinement and chemical adsorption toward polysulfides owing to its abundant pore structure and nitrogen doping. Benefiting from the multiple advantages, the sulfur-nitrogen doped porous carbon/graphene (S-NPC/G) cathode demonstrates a high specific capacity of 1372 mAh $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ as well as good cycling stability for over 300 cycles, indicating the effectiveness of composite structural design of MOFs-derived carbon materials and graphene for high performance Li–S batteries.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The schematic of the fabrication process of NPC/G is shown in Figure 1. Electropositive zinc ions and cobalt ions were added to graphene oxide (GO) to form electrostatic interactions with the electronegative oxygen functional groups on the surface of the GO, followed by the formation of ZIF nanoparticles on graphene oxide (ZIF/GO) after the addition of an organic ligand (2-methylimidazole). In the as-formed ZIF material, the metal atoms are connected with nitrogen atoms in the organic ligand through coordinate bonds, which participate in the formation of a neutral and nanoporous framework consisting of 4-, 6-, and 12-membered-ring $\mathrm{ZnN_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CoN}_{4}$ tetrahedral clusters.[33] The NPC/G hybrid was then obtained after thermal treatment at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The morphology of the ZIF/GO was investigated by transmission electron microscope (TEM) and scanning transmission electron microscope (STEM), as shown in Figure $2\mathsf{a}-\mathsf{c}$ and Figure S1 (Supporting Information). It can be seen that the ZIF nanoparticles, with a diameter of around $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ , are distributed uniformly on the GO sheets. It is worth noting that the morphology of the ZIF/GO is influenced by the molar ratio of zinc and cobalt ions $\mathrm{(Zn/Co)}$ added in the first step of the fabrication process, and the ZIF nanoparticles grown on the GO sheets have sizes ranging from 40  to $630~\mathrm{nm}$ when the molar ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}/$ Co changes from 100:0 to 0:100 (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The STEM image in Figure 2c indicates that ZIF nanoparticles grow on both sides of the GO sheets, which prevents the stacking of GO sheets during thermal treatment. Figure 2d–f and Figure S3 (Supporting Information) show the scanning electron microscope (SEM) image, TEM image, and STEM image of the NPC/G hybrid. It is worth noting that the NPC/G hybrid exhibit the typical wavy and curving structure of graphene without the reservation of the particle morphology of the pristine ZIF (Figure 2a–c). This indicates that the nitrogen-doped porous carbon (NPC) on the graphene sheets exists in a continuous layer, which guarantees intimate contact of the NPC with the graphene conductive scaffold. In the NPC/G hybrid, the graphene provides efficient nucleation sites and act as structure-directing templates. During the thermal treatment, the high surface energy of the ZIF nanoparticles drives them to stick on the graphene sheets, resulting in the formation of a uniform porous carbon layer covered on the graphene sheets. For comparison, Figure S4 (Supporting Information) gives the SEM images of the NPC material synthesized without the presence of graphene, and an aggregation morphology with numerous nanoparticles stacking together was observed, which further indicates the role of graphene in directing the uniform growth of the NPC material.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the ZIF/GO the NPC/G hybrid are shown in Figure 3a. The diffraction peaks corresponding to ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 can be obviously observed in the ZIF/GO,[34] which then disappear in the NPC/G hybrid after thermal treatment, indicating the carbonization of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 with the formation of nitrogen-doped porous carbon. The pore structure of the NPC/G hybrid was studied by the nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms, as shown in Figure 3b. Obvious adsorption in the low pressure region and the hysteresis loop in the medium to high pressure region indicate the presence of micropores and mesopores, respectively.[35] The Brunauer– Emmett–Teller specific surface area is $560~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a broad pore size distribution ranging from 0.7 to $35\mathrm{nm}$ . The micropores enable strong physical adsorption of polysulfides, and the mesopores provide sufficient pathways for lithium ion migration. Compared with the pore structure of reduced graphene oxide (rGO) shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information), the ZIF particles could also prevent the stacking of graphene sheets during thermal treatment. Figure 3c shows the X-ray photoelectron spectroscop (XPS) survey of the NPC/G hybrid, and the signal for nitrogen can be detected with a doping content of $9.6~\mathrm{at\%}$ . The corresponding high-resolution N1s spectrum is shown in Figure 3d, which indicates the presence of three different types of nitrogen, including pyridinic, pyrrolic, and quaternary with binding energies centered at 398.3, 399.7, and $401.0\mathrm{eV.}$ The C1s spectrum in Figure S6 of the Supporting Information also indicates the presence of $\mathrm{C-N}$ bond at a binding energy of $285.8\mathrm{eV.}$ The doped nitrogen endows the NPC/G hybrid with strong polysulfide adsorption capability, contributing to well-localized active materials within the cathode side.[34,36]
Sulfur was impregnated into the NPC/G hybrid by a typical melt-diffusion process[37] at $155~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the S-NPC/G. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves in Figure 4a shows that the sulfur content in the S-NPC/G is $64\mathrm{\mt}\%$ . Raman spectra of the S-NPC/G, NPC/G, and pure sulfur are shown in Figure 4b. The NPC/G hybrid exhibits the characteristic D band at $1329~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and G band at $1586~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of carbon, corresponding to the disordered non- $\cdot\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ stretching mode and ordered graphitic $\displaystyle{\mathrm{sp}}^{2}$ stretching mode,[38] respectively. The intensity ratios of D and G peaks of NPC/G and S-NPC/G are both 1.27, indicating that there is no obvious change of graphene after loading sulfur. It is worth noting that the Raman signals for pure sulfur, which appear below $500~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , were not detected in the S-NPC/G, indicating the uniform distribution of sulfur in the NPC/G matrix.[39] The SEM image of the S-NPC/G is shown in Figure S7 of the Supporting Information, and no obvious sulfur agglomeration can be observed, which is in accordance with the results of Raman spectra. Figure 4c shows the STEM image of the S-NPC/G and Figure $4\mathrm{d-g}$ gives the corresponding elemental maps of carbon, sulfur, nitrogen, and oxygen, which suggest that sulfur has been incorporated into the NPC/G matrix homogenously. The nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherm of the S-NPC/G is shown in Figure S8 of the Supporting Information, and the dramatically decreased specific surface area indicates that the pores within the NPC/G have been occupied by sulfur. The uniformly dispersed sulfur in the NPC/G hybrid guarantees essential electrical contact between the insulating active materials and the conductive scaffold, which promises improved charge transfer kinetics and ensures high sulfur utilization. Moreover, the nitrogen doping sites in the NPC/G hybrid facilitate polysulfide immobilization within the cathode matrix by chemical adsorption, which alleviates irreversible loss of active materials and contributes to good cycling stability.
Coin cells with metallic lithium as the anode were assembled to evaluate the effectiveness of the NPC/G hybrid as a sulfur host material in comparison with the NPC. Figure 5a shows charge/discharge profiles of the S-NPC/G and sulfur-nitrogen doped porous carbon (S-NPC) cathodes at a current density of $0.1\mathrm{{C}}$ ( $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{~\boldmath~\psi~}_{\mathrm{{I}}}\mathrm{C}=1675\mathrm{mAg}^{-1},}\end{array}$ ), in which two discharge plateaus can be obviously observed, corresponding to the reduction of elemental sulfur to long-chain polysulfides ( $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n};$ $n=4–8$ ) at the high potential and further reduction of long-chain polysulfides to lithium sulfides $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)}$ at the low potential.[40] The S-NPC/G electrode delivers a high initial discharge capacity of 1372 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , much higher than that of $893\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ for the S-NPC cathode. It is worth noting that the discharge capacity of the S-NPC/G cathode in the lower discharge plateau is obviously higher than that of the S-NPC cathode. Since the lower discharge plateau corresponds to the formation of solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , good electrolyte accessibility of the electrode is required to complete the liquid to solid reactions. As discussed in Figure 2, graphene serves as a template to direct the uniform distribution of the NPC materials, which enables good electrolyte penetration with shortened ion diffusion paths compared to the NPC agglomerates. In this regard, due to the presence of graphene, the S-NPC/G cathode enables higher utilization of polysulfides involved in the electrochemical conversion reactions during the lower discharge plateau. The cyclic voltammetry profiles of the two cathodes are shown in Figure S9 of the Supporting Information, where two obvious cathodic peaks are observed, which is in accordance with the two discharge plateaus in Figure 5a. The initial charge/discharge profiles of S-NPC/G cathodes with different $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ molar ratio are shown in Figure S10 of the Supporting Information. The S-NPG/G at the molar ratio of 100:1 owns the best electrochemical performance because the thickness of microporous carbon on the surface of graphene is exactly right due to the size of MOFs particles before carbonization, which could provide appropriate path length for utilization of active material. Figure 5b shows the electrochemical impedance spectra of the S-NPC/G and S-NPC cathodes before cycling. The single depressed semicircle at the high frequency region represent the charge transfer resistance, which is an indicator of reaction kinetics of a cell.[41,42] Therefore, the obviously decreased charge transfer resistance of the S-NPC/G cathode indicates a kinetically favored redox process with fast electron and lithium ion transport. Figure 5c shows the long-term cycling performance and corresponding coulombic efficiency of the two cathodes for 300 cycles at a $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ rate. It can be seen that the S-NPC/G cathode exhibits much higher specific capacity than the S-NPC cathode. The rate performances of S-NPC/G and S-NPC cathodes at different rates were shown in Figure 5d. The capacities of S-NPC/G cathode are 1332, 932, 858, and $786~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at the rates of 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, and $^\mathrm{~1~C~}$ , respectively. By contrast, the SNPC electrode exhibited lower discharge capacity and poorer stability under the same conditions. And the capacity of S-NPC/G cathode can achieve $1025\ \mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after returning to $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ , while S-NPC cathode could only give $732\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ . The improved rate capacity of S-NPC/G electrode is attributed to the highly conductive graphene provides an interconnected conductive framework to facilitate fast electron transport. The cycling performance of the S-NPC/G cathode at a high rate of $1\mathrm{~C~}$ was further investigated, as shown in Figure 5e. The cell maintains a specific capacity of $608~\mathrm{{mAh}~\vec{g}^{-1}}$ after 300 cycles, with a low capacity decay of $0.043\%$ per cycle, indicating excellent cycling stability, compared to that of sulfur-reduced graphene oxide (S-rGO) cathode $(0.38\%$ per cycle) shown in Figure S11 of the Supporting Information. The improved electrochemical performance of the S-NPC/G cathode can be ascribed to the multifunctional effects of the NPC/G hybrid: (1) the graphene forms a conductive network for fast electron transport; (2) the uniformly dispersed NPC on the graphene sheets enables good electrolyte penetration, which permits fast lithium ion migration; (3) the pore structure and nitrogen doping of the NPC facilitate both physical confinement and chemical adsorption of polysulfides, resulting in effective suppression of polysulfide diffusion to the anode and contributing to good cycling stabilities.
## 3. Conclusion
We have demonstrated a multifunctional carbon hybrid with MOFs-derived nitrogen-doped porous carbon grown on graphene sheets as a cathode conductive matrix for Li–S batteries. The highly conductive graphene not only facilitates fast electron transport, but also serves as a template for the uniform formation of MOFs-derived carbon. The well-dispersed nitrogendoped porous carbon enables both physical confinement and chemical adsorption toward polysulfides owing to its abundant pore structure and nitrogen doping. As a result, a high initial specific capacity of 1372 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ is achieved, together with a good cycling stability over 300 cycles. This work provides an effective approach for the composite structural design of MOFsderived carbon materials for high performance Li–S batteries and can be extended to other energy storage systems.
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of the NPC/G and the NPC: The NPC/G was synthesized via three steps including the synthesis of ZIF/GO, thermal treatment, and acid pickling processes. GO was synthesized by a modified Hummers method. Typically, $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ and $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (with a molar ratio of 100:1) was dissolved in methanol, and 2-methylimidazole was dissolved in methanol, respectively. GO was dissolved in methanol/ water mixed solvent (with a volume ratio of 4:1) at $1\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ . Next, the above three solutions were mixed together and stirred for $2\ h$ , and the as-obtained mixture solution was aged for $4h$ at room temperature, followed by a freeze drying process for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2d}$ to obtain the ZIF/GO. The ZIF/GO was then annealed under an atmosphere consisting of $10\%H_{2}$ and $90\%$ Ar in a two-step process. In the first step, the ZIF/GO was heated to $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at a heating rate of $5\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ , where it remained for $3h$ ; in the second step, the sample was further heated to $900^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at the same heating rate, where it remained for $4h$ . After thermal treatment, the as-obtained sample was first washed by $50\%$ sulfuric acid and $50\%$ nitric acid (volume ratio 1:1) for several times to remove metal ions, and then washed to neutral by water. After drying at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $12\ h$ , the NPC/G was obtained. The molar ratio of $Z n$ and Co could be changed to 50:50, 1:100, 100:0, and 0:100 for the preparation of control samples. The NPC was synthesized via the same method without the addition of GO. The rGO was obtained from GO used above annealing under an atmosphere consisting of $10\%$ ${\sf H}_{2}$ and $90\%$ Ar in a two-step process.
Preparation of the Sulfur Cathodes: The NPC/G (or NPC, rGO) and sulfur powder in a mass ratio of 1:2 were ground together for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The mixture was then transferred to a sealed stainless steel vessel and heated to $155^{\circ}C$ for $\rceil6\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain the S-NPC/G (or S-NPC, S-rGO). The sulfur cathodes were fabricated by mixing 80 wt% S-NPC/G (or S-NPC, S-rGO), $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ carbon black (Ketjen black EC300J), and $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ polyvinylidene fluoride dissolved in $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone as a slurry, which was then coated onto a carbon-coated Al foil and dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $\mathsf{12h}$ .
Material Characterization: The morphology and structure of the samples were characterized using a SEM (FEI Nova Nano-SEM 430, $\mathsf{15}\mathsf{k V})$ and a TEM (Tecnai F20). XRD patterns were recorded on a Rigaku diffractometer using Cu Ka radiation. Nitrogen adsorption/desorption was determined using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument at $77~\mathsf{K}.$ . Before measurements, the sample powder was degassed at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ until a pressure of $2\mathsf{m m H}\mathsf{g}$ was reached. XPS analysis was performed using an ESCALAB 250 instrument with $\mathsf{A l}\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation (15 kV, 150 W) under a pressure of $4\times10^{-8}\mathsf{P a}$ . TGA was performed with a NETZSCH STA 449 C thermobalance in air with a heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathsf C\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ from room temperature to $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Raman measurements were performed using a Jobin Yvon Lab RAM HR800 with a $632.8~\mathsf{n m}~\mathsf{H e}\cdot\mathsf{N e}$ laser, and the laser spot size was ${\approx}1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
Electrochemical Measurement: 2025-type stainless steel coin cells were assembled inside an $\mathsf{A r}$ -filled glovebox. The sulfur cathode was shaped into a circular pellet with a diameter of $12\ m m$ , and the areal mass density of the sulfur was ${\approx}2.4~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . The electrolyte was prepared by dissolving lithium bistrifluoromethanesulphonylimide (LITFSI, $99\%$ , Acros Organics, 1 m) and lithium nitrate $\mathsf{\Lambda}[\mathsf{L i N O}_{3}$ , $99.9\%$ , Alfa Asea, $0.2\mathrm{~m}^{\cdot}$ ) in 1,2-dimethoxyethane $(99.5\%$ , Alfa Asea), and 1,3-dioxolane $(99.5\%$ , Alfa Asea) (1:1 ratio, by volume). The electrolyte amount added in the cell is $30\upmu\up L$ . Lithium metal foil was used as the anode and Celgard 2400 was used as the separator. A LAND galvanostatic charge–discharge instrument was used to perform electrochemical measurements. The charge/discharge voltage range was $1.7\mathrm{-}2.8~\lor.$ The CV test and EIS measurements were performed using a VSP-300 multichannel workstation.
